==============================================================================
sqlxtc -- design
==============================================================================

This is the in-depth design document for examples/06_sqlxtc, a
networked, concurrent SQL database engine built on libxtc.  README is
the operator's guide (build, run, flags, tests); this document explains
why the pieces are shaped the way they are.

Three companion deep-dives live in ../../docs/:

    the design notes    the from-scratch storage engine
    the design notes      the vectorized executor
    the design notes   MVCC, isolation, and SSI
    the design notes        the write-ahead log and ARIES recovery
    the design notes the clean-slate direction this example took
    M_SQLXTC_EXCISION_PLAN.md  the record of removing SQLite


------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. What sqlxtc is
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

sqlxtc is a complete SQL engine written against the libxtc runtime, and
nothing else.  Its job is to carry a real, demanding workload -- a
multi-client SQL server with durable storage, transactions, isolation,
and crash recovery -- entirely on xtc primitives, because whatever works
here is meant to generalize to a future threaded-PostgreSQL-on-xtc port.

The example began as a fork of SQLite.  The original plan ran two
strategies in parallel: a "hard fork" that routed stock SQLite through
xtc at its documented extension points (mutex, memory, page cache, VFS),
and a "greenfield" build of an xtc-native storage engine underneath the
same engine facade.  The greenfield path won.  The storage engine, a
vectorized executor, and a Lime-generated SQL parser grew until they
covered the entire workload, and then the SQLite code was removed:
sqlite3.c, the VDBE, the virtual-table layer, and all four extension
shims are gone.  The server links no SQLite and contains zero sqlite3
symbols.

The design principles that survived from the SQLite era and shaped the
native engine:

  - The session is the process.  A client connection plus its private
    state (prepared statements, transaction) is one xtc_proc.  There is
    no shared session table and no session mutex.

  - No operation may block the OS thread.  Many sessions run as fibers
    on one (or, with the executor, a few) OS threads, so every blocking
    point -- a latch, a page fault, an fsync -- must park the calling
    fiber and yield the loop, never block the thread.  A thread blocked
    inside one session stalls every peer that shares it.

  - Concurrency is bounded by refusal, not by buffering.  xtc_res caps
    (max clients, max memory, max in-flight queries, max attached
    databases) refuse work at the cap rather than queue it unboundedly;
    that is what keeps p99 latency and resource use predictable.


------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2. Process and concurrency model
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

      accept()                              spawn
    listen_fd ----> listener_proc ----xtc_proc----> conn_proc(fd)
                      (xtc_proc)                        |
                                                        v
                                          read a line -> quack_parse
                                          -> sx_prepare (Lime parse)
                                          -> sx_step (vexec over xstore)
                                          -> quack_emit row stream
                                                        |
                                                        v
                                              send() back to client

  - main.c brings up an xtc_app and a supervisor, applies the xtc_res
    caps, opens the storage engine, and spawns the listener.

  - The listener xtc_proc owns the listening socket (xtc_net, with
    SO_REUSEPORT) and spawns one conn_proc per accepted connection.

  - Each conn_proc is the client's session: its prepared statements, its
    transaction state, and its connection handle are the proc's private
    state.

  - Many conn_procs run as fibers.  With -t 1 they share one loop on one
    OS thread.  With -t N they are spread round-robin by the listener
    across an N-loop executor (N OS threads), so SQL runs in parallel
    across cores.  The parking primitives below are what make a session
    blocked on a latch or on disk yield the thread to its peers instead
    of stalling them.

All connections share one process-global storage engine: its B-link
tree, cooling buffer pool, commit clock, and write-ahead log are shared.
The concurrency comes from inside the engine -- the tree's per-node
latches and the WAL's group-commit writer -- so there is no global
handle mutex on the hot path.  A file-backed database may also be opened
with a private handle per connection (--no-shared), but the data still
lives in the one shared engine.


------------------------------------------------------------------------------
3. The Quack wire protocol
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Quack (see PROTOCOL.md) is a deliberately tiny, line-delimited JSON
protocol over TCP, named in homage to DuckDB but not interoperable with
it.  A request is one JSON object per line:

    {"q": "SELECT id, v FROM t WHERE id > ?1", "params": [10]}

On connect the server sends a hello banner; for each request it streams
one row object per result row, terminated by a status object.  quack.c
is a hand-rolled encoder/decoder with no JSON-library dependency,
because the protocol surface is small and a network-facing parser must
be auditable.  Bound parameters of every value type are supported:
integer, real, text, NULL, and blob (hex or base64 on the wire).

The protocol is intentionally minimal: it demonstrates the
per-connection proc pattern and the engine facade without the weight of
a real wire protocol.  A PostgreSQL-wire front end is a separate, larger
exercise.


------------------------------------------------------------------------------
4. The engine facade
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

engine.h / engine.c define the sx_ surface: sx_open, sx_close, sx_exec,
sx_prepare / sx_step / sx_reset / sx_finalize, the sx_bind_* and
sx_column_* families, sx_errmsg, sx_changes, plus the global lifecycle
(sx_init, sx_shutdown) and the storage-engine controls (sx_storage_open,
sx_storage_run, sx_storage_checkpoint, sx_storage_close).

The entire application speaks sx_.  main.c, conn.c, db.c, quack.c, and
metrics.c never touch the engine internals; engine.c is the boundary.
That boundary is what made the SQLite removal a localized change rather
than a rewrite of the server: the application did not move while the
backend underneath sx_ was replaced.

A connection is a native handle (struct sx_native_db, magic-tagged).
sx_open returns one bound to the global storage engine; sx_open_bt binds
one to a caller-provided B-tree (the in-process storage tests use this).
There is no SQLite object behind the handle.

sx_prepare classifies the statement and parses it.  Reads and writes
recognized by the executor become native statements; sx_step drives the
executor and walks the result.  DDL (CREATE / DROP / ALTER TABLE,
CREATE / DROP VIEW), transaction control (BEGIN / COMMIT / ROLLBACK,
SAVEPOINT / RELEASE / ROLLBACK TO), and PRAGMA table_info are recognized
natively against the engine catalog.  Because the catalog is the only
source of truth -- there is no second schema to fall back to -- a
statement the engine does not recognize is a hard error, not a handoff.


------------------------------------------------------------------------------
5. The SQL front end: parser and vectorized executor
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

5.1 The Lime parser

The accepted SQL subset is described by a Lime grammar
(sql_grammar.lime).  Lime is an LALR push-parser generator (bundled as a
submodule).  The committed, generated parser tables (sql_parse_gen.c /
.h) are checked into the tree and compiled directly, so an ordinary
build needs neither the submodule nor the Lime binary.  The grammar is
regenerated only when it changes (make sql_parse); continuous
integration regenerates it and fails if the committed copy is not
byte-stable.  sql_parse_drv.c is the push-parser driver that turns the
token stream into an AST, sql_ast.c holds the arena-allocated AST nodes,
and sql_parse.c is the lexer and statement pre-classifier.

5.2 The vectorized executor (vexec.c)

vexec is a DuckDB-style vectorized executor: it works on column-oriented
batches ("morsels") of rows rather than interpreting one row at a time.
It plans directly off the Lime AST.  The capability surface, by
entry point:

  - single-table scan with predicate pushdown and projection;
  - aggregation (COUNT / SUM / AVG / MIN / MAX, GROUP BY, HAVING);
  - ORDER BY / LIMIT / OFFSET, including over a join and a view;
  - INNER and LEFT hash joins, two-table and N-way;
  - scalar and IN (SELECT) subqueries, correlation decided by parsing;
  - derived tables (FROM (SELECT ...)) and CREATE / DROP VIEW expansion;
  - FROM-less constant SELECT;
  - column affinity on INSERT and UPDATE, expression-RHS UPDATE;
  - scalar functions (LENGTH, UPPER, LOWER, ABS, COALESCE, IFNULL,
    TYPEOF), LIKE / GLOB, blob literals;
  - INSERT / UPDATE / DELETE with in-transaction read-your-writes.

Single-table scans can be split into morsels and run in parallel through
xtc_exec when the plan allows it.  Reads see a consistent MVCC snapshot
and record their read set so the commit-time SSI check can detect
write-skew.

The correctness bar is an external differential oracle: a corpus of
queries is run against sqlxtc-server and against python's sqlite3, and
any divergence fails the test.  The oracle is independent of any SQLite
linked into sqlxtc -- there is none -- so it is a true black-box
equivalence check, and it is gated in CI.


------------------------------------------------------------------------------
6. The storage engine
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The storage engine is from scratch and xtc-native.  It draws on
LeanStore (cooling-stage eviction, scan resistance) and on B-link-tree
designs (slotted pages, prefix compression, latch crabbing, range
cursors).

6.1 Buffer manager (bufmgr.c)

A page-id-addressed buffer pool.  A fix resolves a page id to a
resident, pinned frame through a striped-lock page table, faulting the
page in on a miss; this is the path the B-tree uses, its child pointers
being stable page ids.  A page-provider xtc_proc cools cold frames (HOT
-> COOL) and writes the dirty ones out ahead of demand, so reclaiming a
frame is a cheap state flip plus a page-table removal.  A second
xtc_proc, the trickler, paces background writeback.

Page reads and writes are submitted to xtc_aio (io_uring on Linux,
kqueue file-AIO on the BSDs) through xtc_dio_sched, so the reactor
thread never blocks on disk: the calling fiber parks until the
completion arrives and the loop keeps serving peers.  A double-write
buffer guards against torn pages: a page is written to the double-write
area and fsynced before it overwrites its home location, so a crash
mid-write can always be repaired during recovery.

The per-frame content latch is an xtc_arwlock.

6.2 Node and tree (btnode.c, btree.c)

btnode.c is a slotted node with prefix compression: the common prefix of
a page's fence keys is stored once and each slot keeps only the key
suffix plus a 4-byte head for fast comparison.

btree.c is a B-link tree with parallel writers and no global writer
lock.  Writers exclusive-latch-couple ("crab") from the root, keeping a
stack of held frames, release every ancestor above a node a child split
cannot cascade into, and propagate a split upward through the
already-held stack -- never acquiring a latch upward, so the scheme is
deadlock-free.  Readers shared-latch-couple (latch the child before
releasing the parent), so a reader is never split out from under itself.
The B-link right-links let a reader or writer that arrives mid-split
follow the link to the correct page rather than block or restart.

The enabling primitive is xtc_arwlock, a fiber-yielding shared/exclusive
latch: its contended waiters park rather than block the OS thread, so a
latch can be held across a child fix or a page-allocation park without
wedging a cooperative loop -- exactly the property latch coupling needs.
A thread-blocking lock could not back it.

6.3 Write-ahead log and recovery (wal.c, xlog.c)

An ARIES-style write-ahead log.  Each transaction logs its changes
durably before they reach the tree; a group-commit writer (an xtc_proc)
coalesces many committers' fsyncs into one batch and hands back durable,
monotonically increasing LSNs.  xlog.c is the log-record codec.

Recovery runs the ARIES analysis / redo / undo passes: redo is
physiological and page-LSN-gated (replaying an already-applied change is
a no-op), and undo of a loser transaction writes compensation log
records so undo is itself idempotent under a second crash.  A clean
shutdown is detected and trusted, so a clean restart skips replay.  The
log is bounded by checkpointing, which compacts it in place (a checkpoint
record plus a dump of the live tree atomically replace the old log).

6.4 Catalog, MVCC, isolation (xstore.c)

xstore.c is the catalog and the MVCC record store on top of the tree.
Table ids are allocated from a persisted on-disk catalog (reserved
table id 0), so distinct table names never collide and the mapping
survives a crash through the WAL.  Each table's rows are versioned;
storage column 0 is the integer rowid (a table with no explicit primary
key gets a hidden synthetic rowid column).

The engine provides:

  - snapshot isolation with per-connection isolation levels (read
    committed through serializable), and time-travel reads (AS OF a
    commit timestamp);
  - serializable snapshot isolation (SSI): a native scan records its
    reads into both a read set and the SSI registry, and the commit-time
    check rolls the transaction back on a dangerous read-write pivot
    (write-skew);
  - in-transaction read-your-writes: an uncommitted write buffer is
    overlaid on a committed range scan (insert appends newest-wins,
    update replaces, delete hides);
  - savepoints (named, nested), via a level stack on the connection;
  - garbage collection / autovacuum of dead versions, with adaptive
    back-off.

A commit clock provides the timestamps; it is a hybrid logical clock so
ordering survives clock skew between nodes for the eventual scale-out
work.


