7. Build, amalgamation, and tests
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The example builds two ways, both in CI:

  - Against libxtc.a (the normal static library).

  - Against the single-file xtc amalgamation (make amalg): the sqlxtc
    sources are compiled -Iamalg/include, where forwarding stub headers
    resolve every "#include xtc_*.h" to one xtc.h, and link
    sqlxtc-server-amalg against a single xtc.o.  This exercises the
    amalgamation as a real, broad consumer of the runtime.  Both links
    are native; there is no SQLite in either.

Tests are claim-driven and run in-process where possible (no daemon,
which the agent harness cannot drive safely):

  - test_xstore / test_xstore_scan -- SQL on xstore, and the
    storage-native scan with read-your-writes and snapshots.
  - test_isolation / test_savepoint / test_native_txn -- isolation
    levels, SSI, savepoints, and native transaction control.
  - test_wal / test_wal_recover / test_torn_smo / test_wal_compact --
    group commit, crash recovery, torn-structure recovery, log
    compaction.
  - test_recover_undo / test_redo_page / test_inplace_redo --
    the ARIES undo pass with CLRs, page-LSN-gated redo, and end-to-end
    in-place physiological recovery.
  - test_clean_restart / test_steal -- the clean-restart fast path and
    a large transaction that spills to disk and is undone on crash.
  - test_bufmgr / test_bufmgr_mt / test_scan_resist / test_persist /
    test_cursor_resume -- the buffer manager single- and
    multi-threaded, scan resistance, superblock persistence, and the
    latch-releasing resumable cursor.
  - test_btnode / test_btree / test_btree_loop / test_btree_mt /
    test_btree_delete_merge -- the slotted node and the B-link tree
    serially, on a loop with offloaded I/O, under parallel writers and
    readers, and with delete-driven merges.
  - test_admin -- the SHOW PROCESSES introspection command.

Outside the in-process suite:

  - test/sqlxtc/test_sqlxtc_oracle.sh -- the differential oracle against
    python's sqlite3 (the authoritative correctness proof).
  - test/sqlxtc/test_sqlxtc_mt.sh -- a multi-thread load test (many
    concurrent clients against a parallel server).


